<!-- ============================================================
     Windows LegacyHive Local Privilege Escalation Detection Rules
     Vulnerability: LegacyHive ProfSvc zero-day (no CVE assigned as of 2026-07-19)
     Researcher: Nightmare-Eclipse (public PoC released 2026-07-15)
     MITRE ATT&amp;CK Techniques:
       T1068  - Exploitation for Privilege Escalation (TA0004)
       T1112  - Modify Registry (TA0005)
       T1134  - Access Token Manipulation (TA0004)
       T1134.001 - Token Impersonation/Theft (TA0004)
     Rule IDs: 104008-104013
     Author: SecOps Engineer (Purple Team)
     Date: 2026-07-19

     Vulnerability Summary:

     LegacyHive is an unpatched zero-day local privilege escalation
     vulnerability in the Windows User Profile Service (ProfSvc). A
     standard-user attacker can load another user's registry hive
     (including a local admin's) into their own context, enabling
     arbitrary code execution when the target account next logs on.

     Attack chain:
       1. Attacker creates a GUID-named staging directory under C:\ root
          and writes modified ntuser.dat + UsrClass.dat into it.
       2. NT Object Manager symbolic links (NtCreateSymbolicLinkObject,
          NtCreateDirectoryObjectEx) redirect the secondary user's
          Local AppData to the attacker-controlled path.
       3. User Shell Folders\Local AppData in the secondary account's
          ntuser.dat is modified to point to the object manager path.
       4. Attacker triggers secondary-user profile load via
          CreateProcessWithLogonW or LogonUser.
       5. An opportunistic lock (oplock) on the staged hive pauses
          ProfSvc hive loading; attacker swaps symlinks mid-load to
          mount the target admin's UsrClass.dat in the attacker's
          registry namespace.
       6. Attacker modifies entries in the mounted admin hive to
          plant a persistence payload (e.g., COM hijack, shell
          extension) executed when the admin account next logs on.

     Affected systems: Windows 10, 11, Server 2016/2019/2022
     (fully patched as of 2026 July Patch Tuesday). No patch exists.

     CVE status: No CVE assigned as of 2026-07-19.

     Log source prerequisites:
       - Sysmon EventID 11 (FileCreate) - Wazuh SID 61644
         Required for rules 104008 (hive file outside profile) and
         104011 (GUID-named directory at drive root).
       - Sysmon EventID 13 (RegistryValueSet) - Wazuh SID 61645
         Required for rule 104009 (User Shell Folders tampering).
       - Sysmon EventID 1 (ProcessCreate) - Wazuh SID 61603
         Required for rule 104012 (LegacyHive exploit binary IOC).
       - Windows Security EventID 4663 (Object Access) - SID 60103
         Required for rule 104010 (cross-user hive file access).
         Must enable "Object Access" auditing + configure SACL on
         C:\Users\*\ntuser.dat and UsrClass.dat paths.
       - Windows Security EventID 4648 (Explicit Credentials) - SID 60103
         Required for rule 104013 (secondary logon from user-mode process).
         Must enable "Logon/Logoff" auditing.

     Severity tiers:
       10 - Explicit credential logon from user-mode process (context/corr.)
       11 - GUID-named directory created at drive root (staging setup)
       12 - Cross-user hive file access by non-SYSTEM process (suspicious)
       13 - ntuser.dat/UsrClass.dat created outside user profile path (high)
       14 - User Shell Folders Local AppData set to non-standard path (critical)
       15 - Known LegacyHive exploit binary detected (IOC exact match)

     References:
       BleepingComputer 2026-07-15:
         https://www.bleepingcomputer.com/news/security/new-windows-legacyhive-zero-day-exploit-grants-hackers-admin-access/
       SecurityOnline 2026-07-15:
         https://securityonline.info/legacyhive-windows-exploit/
       Rescana Technical Analysis 2026-07-17:
         https://www.rescana.com/post/critical-windows-10-11-and-server-zero-day-legacyhive-exploit-enables-privilege-escalation-via-user-profile-service-vuln
       The Register 2026-07-15:
         https://www.theregister.com/security/2026/07/15/microsofts-serial-tormentor-drops-legacyhive-0-day/5271723
     ============================================================ -->

<group name="privilege_escalation,legacyhive,profsvc_lpe,windows,">

  <!-- ===========================================================
       Rule 104008: Hive file created outside user profile directory
       Sysmon Event 11 (FileCreate) - Wazuh SID 61644

       LegacyHive stages modified ntuser.dat and UsrClass.dat copies
       in a non-standard location (typically a GUID-named directory
       under C:\, %TEMP%, or %ProgramData%) before redirecting
       ProfSvc hive loading to these attacker-controlled files.

       Legitimate Windows operations create ntuser.dat only at:
         C:\Users\[username]\ntuser.dat
       and UsrClass.dat only at:
         C:\Users\[username]\AppData\Local\Microsoft\Windows\UsrClass.dat

       Any creation of these files outside those paths is anomalous
       and strongly indicates LegacyHive staging or a similar hive
       manipulation attack.

       Sysmon FileCreate (Event 11) must be configured to log hive
       file names. Add ntuser.dat and UsrClass.dat to the Sysmon
       TargetFilename include filter.
       =========================================================== -->
  <rule id="104008" level="13">
    <if_sid>61644</if_sid>
    <field name="win.system.eventID">^11$</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.targetFilename" type="pcre2">(?i)(ntuser\.dat|usrclass\.dat)$</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.targetFilename" negate="yes" type="pcre2">(?i)C:\\Users\\[^\\]+\\(ntuser\.dat|AppData\\Local\\Microsoft\\Windows\\UsrClass\.dat)$</field>
    <description>LegacyHive ProfSvc LPE: $(win.eventdata.image) created hive file $(win.eventdata.targetFilename) outside user profile directory on $(win.eventdata.computer)  -  LegacyHive stages a modified ntuser.dat or UsrClass.dat outside C:\Users\{user}\ to redirect ProfSvc hive loading; investigate for zero-day LPE activity (T1068, T1112)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1068</id>
      <id>T1112</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>legacyhive,profsvc_lpe,hive_staging,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       Rule 104009: User Shell Folders Local AppData set to non-standard path
       Sysmon Event 13 (RegistryValueSet) - Wazuh SID 61645

       LegacyHive modifies the secondary user's ntuser.dat offline to
       redirect HKCU\Software\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Explorer
       \User Shell Folders\Local AppData away from %USERPROFILE%\AppData\Local
       to an attacker-controlled object manager path (e.g.,
       \\.\globalroot\BaseNamedObjects\Restricted\...).

       When ProfSvc loads the secondary user's profile, it follows this
       redirected Local AppData path to locate UsrClass.dat, walking
       into the attacker's symlink chain.

       The standard value for Local AppData is one of:
         %USERPROFILE%\AppData\Local
         %LOCALAPPDATA%
         C:\Users\{username}\AppData\Local

       Any write to this registry value that does not match standard
       AppData path patterns is critical and warrants immediate
       investigation.

       Sysmon Registry monitoring must include the User Shell Folders
       key path for Event 13 to fire.
       =========================================================== -->
  <rule id="104009" level="14">
    <if_sid>61645</if_sid>
    <field name="win.system.eventID">^13$</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.targetObject" type="pcre2">(?i)\\Software\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Explorer\\User Shell Folders</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.details" negate="yes" type="pcre2">(?i)(%USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\Local|%LOCALAPPDATA%|CSIDL_LOCAL_APPDATA|C:\\Users\\[^\\]+\\AppData\\Local|C:\\Windows\\|%APPDATA%|^\s*$)</field>
    <description>LegacyHive ProfSvc LPE: $(win.eventdata.image) on $(win.eventdata.computer) modified User Shell Folders registry value $(win.eventdata.targetObject) to non-standard path [$(win.eventdata.details)]  -  LegacyHive redirects Local AppData to an NT object manager path to hijack ProfSvc hive loading; this is the core LegacyHive exploitation step (T1112, T1068)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1112</id>
      <id>T1068</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>legacyhive,profsvc_lpe,registry_tamper,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       Rule 104010: Cross-user hive file access by non-SYSTEM process
       Windows Security Event 4663 (Object Access) - Wazuh SID 60103

       Object-access auditing on C:\Users\*\ntuser.dat and
       C:\Users\*\AppData\Local\Microsoft\Windows\UsrClass.dat
       must be enabled (SACL with Everyone:Access).

       In normal operation, ProfSvc (running as SYSTEM inside
       svchost.exe -k netsvcs) is the only non-owner process that
       reads or writes a user's hive files during profile load/unload.

       Event 4663 fired by a non-SYSTEM, non-service account accessing
       another user's hive file is anomalous and indicative of
       LegacyHive-style cross-account hive manipulation.

       NOTE: this rule will produce false positives on endpoints with
       backup agents (Veeam, Windows Backup) or AV products that scan
       hive files. Tune by whitelisting known backup/AV process names
       in your environment once a baseline is established.
       =========================================================== -->
  <rule id="104010" level="12">
    <if_sid>60103</if_sid>
    <field name="win.system.eventID">^4663$</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.objectName" type="pcre2">(?i)C:\\Users\\[^\\]+\\(ntuser\.dat|AppData\\Local\\Microsoft\\Windows\\UsrClass\.dat)</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.subjectUserName" negate="yes" type="pcre2">(?i)(^SYSTEM$|^LOCAL SERVICE$|^NETWORK SERVICE$|.*\$$)</field>
    <description>LegacyHive ProfSvc LPE: $(win.eventdata.subjectUserName) accessed registry hive file $(win.eventdata.objectName) on $(win.eventdata.computer)  -  cross-user hive access by a non-SYSTEM account is anomalous; ProfSvc (SYSTEM) owns legitimate hive loads; investigate for LegacyHive privilege escalation or credential access (T1068)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1068</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>legacyhive,profsvc_lpe,hive_access,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       Rule 104011: GUID-named directory created at drive root
       Sysmon Event 11 (FileCreate) - Wazuh SID 61644

       LegacyHive's attack setup creates a directory with a GUID name
       (e.g., C:\{A1B2C3D4-E5F6-7890-ABCD-EF1234567890}\) directly
       under the drive root to stage modified hive files. This location
       is chosen to be writable by a standard user (unlike Program Files
       or Windows), while being unusual enough that legitimate applications
       rarely create GUID-named root directories.

       GUID-format directories at drive root are a near-exclusive
       indicator of LegacyHive staging, NTFS junction tricks (GhostTree),
       or similar object-manager-abuse techniques. No standard Windows
       feature creates root-level GUID directories.

       Correlate with rule 104008 (hive file outside profile) to confirm
       LegacyHive vs. other techniques.
       =========================================================== -->
  <rule id="104011" level="11">
    <if_sid>61644</if_sid>
    <field name="win.system.eventID">^11$</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.targetFilename" type="pcre2">^[A-Za-z]:\\{[0-9A-Fa-f]{8}-[0-9A-Fa-f]{4}-[0-9A-Fa-f]{4}-[0-9A-Fa-f]{4}-[0-9A-Fa-f]{12}}\\</field>
    <description>LegacyHive ProfSvc LPE: $(win.eventdata.image) on $(win.eventdata.computer) created GUID-named path $(win.eventdata.targetFilename) at drive root  -  LegacyHive stages attack files in a GUID-named directory under C:\; correlate with rule 104008 (hive file outside profile) and 104009 (User Shell Folders tamper) to confirm exploitation (T1068)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1068</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>legacyhive,profsvc_lpe,hive_staging,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       Rule 104012: Known LegacyHive exploit binary detected
       Sysmon Event 1 (ProcessCreate) - Wazuh SID 61603

       The public PoC released by Nightmare-Eclipse is distributed as
       a standalone binary. Known binary names include: LegacyHive.exe,
       legacyhive.exe, LegacyHiveExploit.exe, and variants following
       the researcher's naming convention. Any execution matching these
       names on a Windows endpoint is a confirmed exploit attempt.

       This is a high-confidence IOC rule (level 15). If a binary
       named after the LegacyHive PoC executes, treat it as an active
       exploitation attempt regardless of user context.
       =========================================================== -->
  <rule id="104012" level="15">
    <if_sid>61603</if_sid>
    <field name="win.eventdata.image" type="pcre2">(?i)(legacyhive|legacy_hive|LegacyHiveExploit|profsvc_hive_exploit|hive_hijack_lpe|nightmare.eclipse)</field>
    <description>LegacyHive ProfSvc LPE: CRITICAL  -  known LegacyHive exploit binary $(win.eventdata.image) executed by $(win.eventdata.user) on $(win.eventdata.computer)  -  Nightmare-Eclipse LegacyHive zero-day PoC detected; no patch exists as of 2026-07-19; initiate immediate incident response and host isolation (T1068)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1068</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>legacyhive,profsvc_lpe,exploit_binary,ioc_match,critical,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       Rule 104013: Explicit credential logon from user-mode process
       Windows Security Event 4648 (Explicit Credentials) - SID 60103

       LegacyHive uses CreateProcessWithLogonW or LogonUser to trigger
       loading the secondary user's profile. This triggers ProfSvc
       to walk the attacker's redirected registry path.

       Event 4648 (A logon was attempted using explicit credentials)
       fires whenever a process uses an alternate credential to start
       a session. In normal operation, this event is produced by:
         - Remote Desktop (mstsc.exe)
         - Scheduled Task Service (svchost)
         - IIS Application Pool identity changes
         - runas.exe

       Event 4648 from a non-system, non-standard process is anomalous
       and may indicate LegacyHive's secondary-user profile load step.
       This is an informational/correlation rule (level 10); use it to
       correlate with rules 104008-104012 rather than alert in isolation.

       Correlate: 4648 (this rule) within 60 seconds of 104008/104009
       on the same endpoint is a strong indicator of active LegacyHive
       exploitation.
       =========================================================== -->
  <rule id="104013" level="10">
    <if_sid>60103</if_sid>
    <field name="win.system.eventID">^4648$</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.processName" negate="yes" type="pcre2">(?i)(\\lsass\.exe$|\\svchost\.exe$|\\winlogon\.exe$|\\LogonUI\.exe$|\\mstsc\.exe$|\\taskhost\.exe$|\\taskhostw\.exe$|\\services\.exe$|\\WinRM\.exe$)</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.targetUserName" negate="yes" type="pcre2">(?i)(^ANONYMOUS LOGON$|^-$|.*\$$)</field>
    <description>LegacyHive ProfSvc LPE: Explicit credential logon (Event 4648) initiated from user-mode process $(win.eventdata.processName) as target user $(win.eventdata.targetUserName) on $(win.eventdata.computer)  -  LegacyHive triggers secondary-user profile load via CreateProcessWithLogonW/LogonUser; correlate with rules 104008-104012 within 60 seconds for LegacyHive confirmation (T1068, T1134)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1068</id>
      <id>T1134</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>legacyhive,profsvc_lpe,explicit_credentials,</group>
  </rule>

</group>
